\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A fixed voltage splits the equations}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{While #{V} is held at a constant, the state stays in that
constant-voltage slice, and #{n}, #{m}, and #{h} each satisfy a
linear equation of their own. The explicit solution recorded with
the [activation equation](fnip-0071) is then the whole trajectory
on that slice, so a clamp record determines the steady value and
the time constant.
\citet{pp. 20–22}{ermentrout2010mathematical} separates the
currents and computes the conductances in this way.}

\p{\citet{draft pp. 41–42}{carnevale2004neuron} says that NEURON's
default CVODE absolute error, #{10\,\mu\mathrm{V}} for the
membrane potential, makes a classical Hodgkin–Huxley action
potential at #{6.3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}} comparable to a second-order
method with fixed step #{\Delta t=25\,\mu\mathrm{s}}.
\citet{Figure 4.18, draft pp. 47–49}{carnevale2004neuron} is a
different comparison, near threshold: a weak current pulse and a
pulse twice as strong. Backward Euler at that same step moves the
latency of the weak spike about as much as a change of one percent in
the peak sodium conductance #{0.12\,\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}}.}

